<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rif</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rif Mountains, Riff</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rif is a mountainous region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, inhabited mainly by Riffian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> who speak Tarifit. It was the home of the early Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekor">Nekor</a> and later of strongholds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a>. From 1912 it lay in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> of 1921–1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-el-krim">Abd el-Krim</a> led a revolt that founded the short-lived Rif Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rif.jpg" alt="Rif" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabi from Alicante, Spain · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chauen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rif is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the west, the Ouergha River to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulouya-river">Moulouya River</a> to the east. It is the homeland of the Riffians, the Senhaja de Srair, the Jebala and the Ghomara. The mountains belong geologically to the Gibraltar Arc and are an extension of the Baetic System of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, so they are not part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> system. The western Rif, often called the Jebala, is largely Arabized and speaks Moroccan Arabic, though the Ghomara keep their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> language. The eastern Rif, the Rif proper, speaks Tarifit.</p><p>The region has more rainfall than any other part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, in places over 2,000 millimetres a year. Its western and central parts are forested with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> cedar, cork oak and holm oak, and hold the only remaining forests of Moroccan fir. Its mountains shelter subpopulations of the endangered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> macaque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> have lived in the Rif since prehistoric times. From the 11th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> founded trading posts and cities on its coast, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Rusadir</a>, now Melilla, and in the 5th century BCE Tingi, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> the coast came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Rif became part of the Kingdom of Mauretania; when Mauretania was divided under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, Tangier became the capital of Mauretania Tingitana. Roman rule ended in the 5th century CE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> later held part of the region. In 710 Salih I ibn Mansur founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekor">Nekor</a> in the Rif, and the Berbers began converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>In the 15th century many Muslims expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> settled in the western Rif, bringing Andalusian music and founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a>. Portugal invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415 and Spain conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a> in 1490. The Hispano-Moroccan War began in 1859 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> and ended in Moroccan defeat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> guerrilla leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-el-krim">Abd el-Krim</a> proclaimed the Republic of the Rif in 1921, and the Riffians won several victories over Spain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> before they were defeated; Spanish forces used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> extensively. Spain restored its zone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in April 1956, a month after the French zone became independent. The 1958 Rif riots followed, and the Moroccan government suppressed the uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Rif is divided into an eastern part around Nador, Driouch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hoceima">Al Hoceima</a> and a western part around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tétouan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a> and Taounate. Farmers there grow most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s cannabis. Since the 1950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a>, forest fires and clearing for agriculture have caused heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> and soil loss.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
